# Runtime data — never commit live results or secrets.
# Allowlist pattern: everything under files/ is ignored except the tracked
# example. This covers newer runtime files (offsets.json, field-colors.json,
# theme-colors.json, sessions.json, legacy admin.json) automatically instead
# of relying on a hand-maintained per-file list.
files/*
!files/schedule.example.json

# Demo sandbox runtime data (recreated from demo-seed/ on every reset).
# demo-seed/ itself IS tracked — it's the canonical demo snapshot.
# Track the directory placeholder so git clones create demo-files/ with
# user-owned permissions; Docker bind mounts then re-use the existing dir
# instead of creating a new root:root one that the node user can't write to.
demo-files/*
!demo-files/.gitkeep

# Rolling database backups written by the `backup` compose service. These are
# full dumps: they contain bcrypt password hashes and hashed team tokens, so
# they must never be committed.
backups/

# The live database and the generated secrets. These are HOST DIRECTORIES
# rather than Docker named volumes precisely so `docker compose down -v`
# cannot delete them — see the note at the bottom of compose.yml. The rolling
# dumps in ./backups (above) are the second copy; restore one with
# scripts/db-restore.sh.
pgdata/
secrets/

# Docker and system noise
# .env holds per-machine SCALARS (TT_PORT, PUID/PGID, secrets).
# compose.override.yml holds per-machine STRUCTURE (an extra volume, a debug
# port, a different image tag). Compose merges it automatically when present.
# Between them there is no reason to edit the tracked compose.yml on a
# deploy box — and not editing it is what keeps `git pull` from stopping.
.env
compose.override.yml
docker-compose.override.yml
*.log
node_modules/

# Editor/OS noise
.DS_Store
.vscode/
*.swp
*.bak
frontend/icons/source_*.png
